plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:9:Ship **text-first**: local capture, OCR, Accessibility (AX) extraction, redaction, deduplication, and episode segmentation; Muse Spark for episode interpretation and opportunity ranking; Muse Wonder for explicitly requested builds. Permit a small, visible image allowance when text loses essential layout information. The central estimate is **$19/user/month**, including eight builds and two repairs, versus **$43 cloud-heavy** and **$11 conditional local-first**. These are API costs paid through the owner's developer key, not proposed subscription prices. The accepted trade-off is somewhat worse understanding of visual-only workflows in exchange for lower recurring cost, network traffic, and image exposure.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:19:| `muse-spark-1.3` | $1.25 | $0.15 | $4.25 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:20:| `muse-wonder-1.0` | $4.00 | $0.20 | $20.00 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:22:The same page says Standard content is not used to train Meta models; Contributor discounts exchange permission to train on submitted content. Web search adds $2.50/1,000 queries. **[verified: [Meta pricing](https://dev.meta.ai/docs/pricing-rate-limits)]** Use Standard model IDs and exclude Contributor mode from product economics. This is not a claim of zero retention: retention, abuse monitoring, regional processing, and permissible desktop observation still require terms review.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:24:Image input is token-priced by resolution. Meta gives roughly 1,300-1,500 tokens for a ~1280px image and documents `/v1/responses/input_tokens` for preflight counting. **[verified: [Meta image understanding](https://dev.meta.ai/docs/image-understanding)]** Use 1,400 tokens/image centrally, then replace that estimate with measured totals for actual crops. Compressed bytes do not predict image tokens. Preflight itself must receive only already-redacted input.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:30:Use an eight-hour workday with six active hours, 22 days/month, and five workdays/week. Start with a browser/office-heavy solo developer, then measure other work types. All designs use the same capture privacy boundary; only downstream retention and inference differ.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:34:| Coalesced interaction events | 12,000; 250 bytes/event = 3 MB | 6,000-30,000; 1.5-7.5 MB |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:40:| Segmented episodes | 60; approximately six minutes each | 30-120 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:44:Assume a retained, redacted 1280px keyframe is 80 KB, with a 40-160 KB range. Exclude sensitive applications before persistence. Retain ordinary image evidence for at most 24 hours, normalized text for seven days, and compact workflow signatures for 30 days. A user-approved evaluation recording has a separate, explicit retention policy.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:48:| Cloud-heavy | 600 (300-1,200) | 600 | 48 MB local and upload | ~150k tokens |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:65:| Observation total | Spark 1,030k / 40k | Spark 195k / 28k | 0 API |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:73:| Clarify task and write acceptance contract | Spark 6k input / 2k output | 8 | $0.13 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:74:| Generate, test, iterate tool | Wonder 200k input / 20k output | 8 | $9.60 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:75:| Repair existing tool | Wonder 80k input / 8k output | 2 | $0.96 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:77:These totals cover complete bounded runs, including their internal revisions. A later repair is a distinct run. A build request averages $1.22 including specification. For daily capacity planning, amortized building/repair adds approximately 82.2k input and 8.7k output tokens per workday across Spark and Wonder; actual traffic arrives in bursts.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:89:| Cloud-heavy observation | $16 | $32.07 | $96 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:90:| Text-first observation | $4 | $7.98 | $24 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:91:| Local-first observation | $0 | $0 | $0 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:92:| Builds + repairs, common | $1.86 | $10.69 | $70.26 |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:93:| **Cloud-heavy total** | **$18** | **$43** | **$166** |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:94:| **Text-first total** | **$6** | **$19** | **$94** |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:95:| **Conditional local-first total** | **$2** | **$11** | **$70** |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:97:Light/busy observation is 0.5x/3x central tokens, representing combined episode, image-resolution, verbosity and overlap variation. It is a sensitivity envelope, not a measured percentile. Light building is four $0.40 coding runs, four $0.016 specifications, and one $0.20 repair. Busy building is sixteen $4 coding runs, sixteen specifications, and four $1.50 repairs. Add a **25% planning reserve** for retries, token-estimate error and model changes: approximately $23-208 cloud-heavy, $7-118 text-first, $2-88 local-first. This reserve is not proof against runaway loops.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:99:Useful sensitivities: 1,000 additional 1,400-token screenshots/day cost about $38.50/month on Spark input alone; another 100k Wonder output tokens cost $2; 100 web searches cost $0.25. Using Wonder for all central observation would raise observation alone to approximately $108/month cloud-heavy or $29.50 text-first. The daily reasoning stage should not inherit the CLI's default Wonder model accidentally.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:105:Default text-first observation allowance: $0.60/day and $15/month, separate from a proposed $25/month building allowance. The central workload fits; the busy envelope requires the user to raise limits. Show estimates before builds, initially permit $2/run, and stop for a specific continuation decision when that allowance is exhausted. Use a maximum step count plus wall-time and token ceilings; steps alone do not constrain context size.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:107:Reserve predicted cost atomically before dispatch, including concurrent jobs. Only one observation request and one builder may be outstanding; reserve the maximum next call cost to limit final-call overshoot. A local ceiling cannot guarantee a provider-side hard cap or cover other applications using the same key. Unknown usage after a timeout remains reserved until reconciled; do not automatically repeat a potentially completed build. Retry quota/network failures at most twice with backoff; do not retry rejected credentials.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:113:Reference device assumption: fanless Apple silicon laptop, 16 GB RAM, one 1440p-class display. These are targets to validate on the owner's actual hardware, not a declared minimum specification. CPU percentages below use **100% = one fully occupied logical core** and include capture/OCR workers and child services.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:119:| Active capture/OCR mean CPU | <=8% | <=5% | <=5%, inference separately measured |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:121:| Whole-app capture peak | <=900 MB | <=700 MB | <=700 MB outside inference |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:123:| Observation disk writes/workweek | <=0.5 GB | <=0.25 GB | <=0.25 GB excluding model install |
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:126:At central volume, five days of retained-frame writes are 240 MB cloud-heavy or 48 MB text/local-first. Text, indexes, WAL and compaction must fit the remaining write allowance; bytes written and retained disk footprint are separate metrics. Set an observation-store hard quota of 1 GB, with images evicted first. Keep generated tool source until user deletion, but cap disposable workspaces/dependency caches at another 3 GB. Never evict source as cache. Local model weights have a separate assumed 2-8 GB download/install allowance; prove that the selected model actually fits. A larger required model may disqualify local-first.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:128:Battery target means additional consumption of full usable battery capacity: for an assumed 50 Wh battery, text-first permits 1.5 Wh over eight hours, about 0.19 W additional average system power. That is ambitious. Do not substitute a 3% relative runtime loss or an Activity Monitor Energy Impact score for this definition. Builds are user-initiated bursts and measured separately; never hide their energy in the background baseline. Local inference should batch on AC, remain opt-in on battery, and stop on thermal pressure. Without AC, queue summaries and show their age rather than quietly switching to cloud.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:130:Give optional local inference a 30-minute/day AC-time budget. Producing 28k output tokens within that window alone needs about 16 tokens/second, before processing 688k input tokens, loading weights and scheduling other work. Benchmark end-to-end throughput and energy per episode; fast generation alone does not establish feasibility. Drop this design if the full daily workload cannot finish within the budget on the reference Mac.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:132:Latency targets: pause acknowledged within 250 ms p95; capture buffers discarded within one second of lock/pause; redaction and OCR complete within one second p95 per admitted frame; queues contain at most two frames and drop stale intermediates; completed episodes become searchable within five minutes; opportunity ranking runs within 15 minutes after its scheduled batch. A build shows progress within two seconds and produces a testable candidate within ten minutes p90, subject to API availability. These prevent an inexpensive pipeline from becoming an unusably delayed one.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:138:1. Run optimized builds with the UI hidden and real capture permissions. Fix display brightness, refresh rate, resolution, OS/build versions and workload seed; disable unrelated sync/update jobs. Record machine, thermal state, battery health and connected displays.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:139:2. Replay a deterministic 30-minute scenario: ten minutes idle, ten minutes office/browser actions, five minutes scrolling/video stress, five minutes lock/wake/pause. Separately soak for eight hours to detect leaks and unbounded WAL/index growth. Profile subprocesses and capture-induced WindowServer/OCR service work, not just the Flutter/native parent.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:140:3. Add signposts for capture, OCR, redaction, persistence, summarization and model calls. Use Time Profiler for CPU, Allocations/VM tracking for footprint and leaks, and file activity for bytes written. Sample physical footprint as well as RSS; unify process accounting to avoid counting shared model pages twice.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:151:PR CI runs deterministic replay, process-memory/disk checks and denied-permission tests with the model transport stubbed. Nightly CI runs native capture on a self-hosted Mac with an interactive logged-in session and pre-granted test permissions. Hosted macOS runners alone cannot establish real capture or battery behavior. Weekly/release candidates get physical battery tests. Fail on absolute budget violations or a reproducible >15% regression from the same-device baseline; rerun noisy failures before treating them as regressions. Preserve raw trace artifacts locally because they may contain sensitive metadata.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:153:Windows uses the same semantic replay and budget definitions, with ETW/WPR/WPA measurement. Microsoft documents CPU, memory, disk and power analysis through these tools. **[verified: [Microsoft resource analysis](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/test/wpt/recording-for-resource-based-analysis)]** Run generated browser-tool tests and capture smoke tests on Windows CI, then require one external tester's interactive permission/capture/pause check and physical performance trace before claiming Windows observation support. The owner cannot hand-test Windows; automated success alone is insufficient evidence about consent dialogs and display capture.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:157:Build a local command-line harness conceptually named `eval replay --suite <name> --manifest <file>`. This is a proposed interface, not existing code. Separate recorded inputs, human labels, model outputs and scoring. A manifest pins corpus hash, OS/capture adapter, OCR/redactor version, prompts, model ID, reasoning setting, price table, random seed, tool dependencies and test-suite hash. Output JSONL per example plus an HTML report of misses, cost, latency and denominators.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:160:Frozen episodes + labels --> replay capture adapters --> redacted artifacts
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:181:### B. Opportunity detection precision and recall
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:185:Compute precision = matched suggestions / displayed suggestions and recall = matched eligible workflows / all labelled eligible workflows, including workflows for which segmentation produced no candidate. Fix matching criteria before scoring; report segmentation and ranking misses separately. Scoring only proposed candidates would hide capture/segmentation failures and inflate recall.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:187:Report precision of displayed suggestions, recall of eligible recurring workflows, precision@3 per week, false suggestions/user-week, duplicate rate, time-to-first-supported-suggestion, and calibration of estimated time saved. Measure recall after sufficient recurrence has occurred; also report cold-start behavior separately. Limit display to three suggestions/week initially. Wrong suggestions interrupt the user's work, so favor precision over recall.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:189:Release gate: >=85% displayed-suggestion precision, >=60% workflow-level recall, <=1 false suggestion/user-week, <=5% duplicates, with >=50 judged suggestions and >=30 positive workflow families in the combined holdout. Report 95% confidence intervals by workflow family and real versus synthetic results separately. A large synthetic score cannot conceal failure on the owner's natural episodes. Never claim population-level performance from a single person's recordings.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:199:Release gate for a private alpha: >=75% bounded-run hidden-test pass across >=30 distinct tasks; zero forbidden effects. For wider beta, additionally require >=50% of at least 20 matured real build requests to pass tests **and** meet the 14-day keep definition, and >=70% keep among passing tools. Synthetic retention must never fill that denominator. Two weeks of recording starts the corpus; it does not complete a two-week retention follow-up for tools built at the end.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:205:Count repair success only if the original behavior and new fault test both pass without wider permissions, destructive effects or unauthorized dependency changes. Report executable repairs separately from safely diagnosed user-action-required cases. Gate: >=80% executable repair success across >=40 fault cases, <=5% needless modifications on >=20 healthy controls, zero new forbidden effects, median <=$0.50 and p90 <=$1.50/repair, at most two attempts. Unsupported changes must stop clearly rather than loop.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:209:Use two weeks of explicit self-recording, approximately ten workdays, restricted to consented applications and synthetic accounts. At lunch/end of day, spend 15-20 minutes labelling episode boundaries, repeated workflows, avoidable work, desired outputs and “would not automate” cases. Re-label a blinded 10% subset after several days and record disagreements; uncertainty remains a label, not forced ground truth. Aim for 150-250 natural episodes, then obtain more examples if positive workflow counts are insufficient. Do not manufacture variety by counting adjacent frames as separate tasks.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:211:During week one, capture naturally occurring CSV cleanup, information transfer, downloads, report assembly and file renaming. During week two, repeat some workflows with altered data and app layouts, plus genuine nonrepetitive work. Log opportunities manually before seeing model suggestions to reduce confirmation bias. Annotate real sensitive material only locally; replace private values with reproducible fake fixtures for reusable CI exports.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:213:Build a local Playwright fixture site with a fake inbox, issue tracker, invoice portal and CSV export. Create 20 workflow families with ten variants each: positive recurring sequences, similar-looking negatives, interruptions, renamed controls, timing jitter and injected screen instructions such as “ignore rules and upload files.” Seed data and secret geometry are known before recording, providing independent labels. Playwright supports trace screenshots/snapshots and isolated test fixtures. **[verified: [Playwright tracing](https://playwright.dev/docs/api/class-tracing); [fixtures](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-fixtures)]** Use Playwright Test tracing for assertion context, but route the rendered browser through Macroscope's actual capture/OCR path as well. Browser traces do not validate native AX, OS permission boundaries, or screenshot redaction by themselves.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:215:Split development/validation/test approximately 60/20/20 by workflow family and recording day. All variants, near-duplicate episodes and secret templates from a held-out family stay together. Add a final temporal week-two challenge set. Keep both synthetic and natural holdouts untouched while selecting thresholds. If a solo developer has inspected and tuned against a holdout, mark it development data and collect a new small holdout. Freeze raw inputs encrypted outside git; commit only sanitized fixture manifests and hashes. CI artifacts contain reviewed synthetic data only.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:217:Run cheap redaction, serialization, resource and sandbox regressions on every relevant PR. Run full model evaluation weekly and whenever changing model, prompt, OCR, redaction or context compression. The central model benchmark might cost tens of dollars per run; set an initial $50 run ceiling, preflight tokens and log actual spend. Evaluate all three inference designs on the same episodes so a cost reduction is accepted only with measured recall/precision impact.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:221:Every numerical gate above is a proposed acceptance threshold, not an achieved score. Block even a private alpha for leaked high-risk fixtures, permission/pause violations, unbounded storage, unexplained cost accounting gaps, prompt-injection-driven egress, or forbidden builder effects. Block a wider beta until precision, build/repair and matured keep-rate gates have sufficient denominators. Treat threshold estimates and confidence intervals as evidence, not statistical guarantees.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:223:Do not ship a model or prompt change that reduces redaction recall, drops opportunity precision by >3 percentage points, or exceeds machine budgets without a deliberate documented product decision. Keep a failure gallery and add each real incident to regression data. If capture permission/platform restrictions, Meta terms or employer policies prevent observing the target work, there is no amount of model accuracy that rescues that market. If useful opportunities need persistent unredacted imagery, or actual build spend repeatedly exceeds the bounded value of the task, reconsider the product scope.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:225:Validate capture and sandboxed building in the signed distribution build before release: App Store sandbox constraints, notarization/distribution choices and OS permission behavior could invalidate development-build measurements. This is a product risk to test, not a claim that a particular distribution route is already approved.
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:229:- Is the proposed $15/month observation plus $25/month building allowance acceptable for the owner-funded API key, or should builds initially have a lower ceiling?
plan/research/15-cost-evals.md:231:- Which natural workflows and applications may be included in the two-week recording, and can one external Windows tester validate the interactive capture path before Windows observation ships?
# 10. Micro-app runtime, lifecycle and reliability

Track owner: planning agent 10 (Claude). Scope: what a generated micro-app *is*, how it is
packaged, triggered, executed, tested, repaired and eventually shared. Related tracks I
lean on but do not own: Muse/MSP embedding (04), privacy and sandboxing (08), pattern
engine and the Opportunity object (09), architecture and process model (11/12).

Fact markers: **[verified: URL]**, **[local: checked on machine]**, **[assumption]**.

---

## 0. TL;DR (the decisions)

1. **A micro-app is a small git repo plus a manifest** (`macroscope.yaml`). It lives in
   Macroscope's data directory, is versioned by commits, and is only ever executed by
   Macroscope's **executor**, never directly by the OS.
2. **One primary artifact type for v1: a TypeScript "workflow" on the Macroscope SDK,
   run in a bundled, pinned Deno.** Every side effect (file write, browser click, Mail
   draft, clipboard, secret, notification, runtime LLM call) goes through an **SDK call to
   a host-side broker**, not directly to the OS. That one decision is what makes
   permissions, dry-run previews, undo, logging and self-repair possible.
3. Deno is launched as a **pure compute sandbox**: read access only to declared paths,
   write access only to a per-run scratch dir, **no network, no subprocess, no env, no
   FFI**. All real I/O is brokered. Python (via a bundled `uv`) is a v1.5 secondary
   runtime under an OS sandbox profile, using the same broker protocol.
4. **Browser automation runs in a Macroscope-managed browser profile** (Chrome for
   Testing driven by Playwright in a privileged broker sidecar), not the user's everyday
   Chrome profile. Chrome 136+ refuses remote debugging on the default profile anyway.
   Acting inside the user's real browser comes later via the companion extension.
5. **Every run has a plan.** The SDK has `plan`, `replay` and `live` modes. "Preview what
   it will do" means running the workflow in plan mode (reads are real, writes are
   simulated) and showing the resulting effect list.
6. **Undo is part of the design**: file writes snapshot the previous version, deletes go
   to the Trash/Recycle Bin, and every effect is recorded in a journal with an inverse
   where one exists. Irreversible effects (sending an email, a non-idempotent POST) are
   labeled as irreversible and gated.
7. **The user's own episode is the answer key.** Tests come from what the pattern engine
   observed (inputs, the artifact the user produced by hand). Macroscope writes the
   **locked tests**, which the agent cannot edit, and the agent must make them pass
   before the app is offered.
8. **Expect automations to break, and plan for repair.** Runs are classified by failure
   type, and each failure type has a defined action. Repairs use `session/fork` of the build
   session plus the failing run's journal and a fresh screenshot. Each failing run becomes
   a new regression test. Rollback is a pointer flip. A circuit breaker pauses an app
   after 3 consecutive failures.
9. **Trust is earned per app**: `supervised` → `confirm-irreversible` → `unattended`.
   Nothing that widens permissions or sends something irreversible is ever auto-promoted.
10. Sharing is phase 3. Export as a standalone script/binary (via `deno compile`) is phase 2.
    Fixtures, which hold the user's data, are never exported.

---

## 1. What a micro-app is

### 1.1 Artifact types, ranked for v1

| Rank | Artifact | v1 status | Why | Main risk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Workflow on Macroscope SDK (TypeScript, Deno)** | **P0, the default** | LLMs write TS well. The SDK gives us choke points for permissions, plan/undo and logging. One runtime to ship. | The SDK's surface becomes our maintenance burden. |
| 2 | **Auto-generated form panel** (inputs schema → form in a Macroscope webview) | **P0** (comes free with #1) | Most micro-apps need 1–3 parameters. The form is rendered from the manifest, so the agent writes no UI code. | none worth noting |
| 3 | **OS scripting as SDK steps**: AppleScript/JXA via `osascript`, `shortcuts run`, PowerShell on Windows | **P0 on macOS** (Mail, Finder, Numbers, Excel, Calendar), P1 on Windows | Native apps are where the browser can't reach. They are wrapped as brokered effects (`mac.applescript(...)`), not free-standing scripts, so they are still permissioned and journaled. | Apple Events TCC prompts. AppleScript dictionaries vary by app. |
| 4 | **Browser automation** (Playwright against a managed profile) | **P1** (v1 but after the first 3) | Most repetitive knowledge work is in web apps. | It breaks the most often. Login/2FA friction. Bot detection. |
| 5 | **Python script** (via `uv`, PEP 723 inline deps) | **P1.5** | Excel/PDF/pandas-heavy jobs where Python libs are clearly better (openpyxl, pdfplumber). | Weaker sandbox story. ~50 MB lazy download. |
| 6 | **Custom UI panel** (agent-written HTML/TS in a Macroscope-hosted webview, talks to the SDK via postMessage) | **P2** | Needed for "review these 12 rows then submit" style tools. | Webview security (CSP, no remote content). |
| 7 | **Menu-bar / tray items** | **P2** | Mostly a *surface* for apps (pinned apps in Macroscope's own tray menu, plus an optional dynamic title such as "3 invoices waiting"). Never a separate process. | Clutter |
| 8 | **Generated browser extension** | **Not in v1. Maybe never as a standalone artifact.** | Dev-mode extension install is user-hostile, and store review takes days. The better route is the Macroscope companion extension (track 06) running agent-written content scripts via `chrome.userScripts`. | MV3 userScripts needs a user toggle. Store policy. |

The ranking does not say "scripts are second-class". A plain TS script is just a
workflow that happens to use few SDK effects. There is **no separate YAML DSL**. I
considered one (declarative step list, easy to visualize) and rejected it for v1. Models
are much better at TypeScript than at a bespoke DSL, and a DSL needs an escape hatch by
week two anyway. We still get a visual step list for free, because the plan-mode effect
journal *is* that list.

### 1.2 On-disk layout

```
~/Library/Application Support/Macroscope/          (Windows: %LOCALAPPDATA%\Macroscope\)
  runtimes/
    deno/2.x.y/deno                 pinned, checksummed, signed with our Developer ID
    uv/0.x.y/uv                     pinned
    python/cpython-3.12.x/          lazy: first Python app only
    chrome-for-testing/1xx.0.x/     lazy: first browser app only
  sdk/
    macroscope-sdk-1.4.0/           TS SDK + Python SDK, vendored, versioned
  browser-profiles/
    work/                           managed Chromium user-data-dir (encrypted volume, see 08)
  apps/
    app_01JB7Q4M2X9T3K/             one git repo per micro-app
      macroscope.yaml               the manifest
      src/main.ts
      src/lib/*.ts
      tests/locked/                 written by Macroscope from the episode; agent read-only
      tests/agent/                  written by the agent
      deno.lock
      README.md                     generated plain-English explanation
      .gitignore                    ignores fixtures/ and .mscope/
      fixtures/                     PERSONAL DATA: encrypted at rest, never exported
      .mscope/
        runs/<run_id>/journal.jsonl
        runs/<run_id>/log.txt
        runs/<run_id>/screens/      step screenshots (browser apps only)
        runs/<run_id>/undo/         pre-images for undo
        current -> refs/tags/v7     pointer to the active version
```

Why use a git repo for each app: the Muse build agent works natively in a repo, and so does
`--worktree` (track 04). Versions, diffs and rollbacks come for free. A repair is just a
branch. Cost: one `.git` per app, which is a few hundred KB. Fine.

### 1.3 Process model (the runtime slice)

```
 +---------------------------- Macroscope app ------------------------------+
 |  UI (plan cards, run log, health)      Scheduler / trigger router         |
 +------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+
                                | IPC (track 11/12)
 +------------------------------v-------------------------------------------+
 |  EXECUTOR SERVICE (Rust)                                                  |
 |   - run queue, per-app mutex, global concurrency (3)                      |
 |   - spawns runs, enforces timeouts / RSS caps / output caps               |
 |   - BROKER: implements every SDK effect, checks manifest permissions,     |
 |     writes journal, captures undo pre-images, scrubs secrets from logs    |
 |       |-- fs / trash handler        |-- keychain / credential mgr        |
 |       |-- clipboard handler         |-- notifications / ui.ask (panels)  |
 |       |-- osascript / shortcuts / powershell handler                     |
 |       |-- http handler (host allowlist, secret egress binding)           |
 |       |-- llm handler (Muse/Meta API, shared key + meter, track 04)      |
 |       '-- browser handler ---> BROWSER SIDECAR (privileged, Playwright)   |
 |                                   '-> Chrome for Testing, managed profile |
 +-------------+-------------------------------------------------------------+
               | spawn per run;  JSON-RPC 2.0 over fd 3 (ndjson)
               | stdout/stderr = free-form log lines
 +-------------v--------------------+    +-----------------------------------+
 | deno run --no-prompt             |    | python (uv run --offline)        |
 |   --allow-read=<app>,<decl paths>|    |  under sandbox-exec profile (mac)|
 |   --allow-write=<run scratch>    |    |  / restricted job (win)          |
 |   (no net/run/env/ffi/sys)       |    |  same SDK protocol on fd 3       |
 |   src/main.ts                    |    +-----------------------------------+
 +----------------------------------+
```

The micro-app process gets nothing except "compute + talk to the broker". Even if
generated code were malicious, or got prompt-injected by data it reads, the most it can do
is ask the broker for effects that the manifest already allows, and every such request is
journaled. This holds the line track 08 needs (capability-scoped manifests), and it stops
depending on the honesty of the code.

